.TH COW 1
.SH NAME
cow, spots \- run a command on a copy of this directory, and show what it changed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cow
.I dir
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
.PP
.B spots
.I dir
[
.I base
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cow
runs
.I cmd
in a new name space where
.IR cowfs (4)
serves the current directory, with every change kept in
.IR dir ,
so that the real files do not change.
.I Dir
may be relative to the current directory;
.I cow
makes it if it does not exist.
.I Cow
refuses a
.I dir
that holds anything, or one inside the current directory.
The command's standard output and error are its own:
.I cow
prints only its own errors.
It keeps the changes whatever the command's status, and exits with that status.
.PP
.I Cow
serves only the current directory and what lies below it.
A command that changes to a directory outside it, or that names a file there, works on the real
files.
.PP
.I Spots
prints the changes kept in
.I dir
as a patch for
.IR base ,
or the current directory without one, which must be the tree
.I cow
served.
For each file that the command made, removed, or changed, it prints Git's header,
.BI "diff --git a/" path " b/" path \f1,
then
.B new file mode
or
.B deleted file mode
for a file the command made or removed,
or
.B old mode
and
.B new mode
when the execute bit changed,
and then the unified diff,
against
.B /dev/null
for a file made or removed.
A mode is
.B 100755
when the owner may execute the file, and
.B 100644
otherwise.
The header alone shows an empty file made or removed.
A file changed and then changed back, or made and then removed, does not appear, and
directories appear only through their files.
A file removed and a file made with the same text, which is not empty, show as a rename:
.BI "diff --git a/" old " b/" new \f1,
then the mode lines if the execute bit changed,
.BR "similarity index 100%" ,
.BI "rename from " old \f1,
and
.BI "rename to " new \f1,
with no diff.
Each removed file, in the order of their paths, takes one made file:
of those with its text, the one with the same last element, then the one whose path shares the
longest start with its own, then the first in the order of their paths.
.I Spots
quotes a name holding a control character, a double quotation mark or a backslash as Git does, in
double quotation marks with C's escapes.
.I Spots
leaves out Git's
.B index
line, so
.B patch -p1
and
.B git apply
both take its output.
It leaves out of the patch each binary file whose text changed, one with a NUL byte anywhere in
either version, naming it on standard error as
.BI "spots: binary: " path \f1;
the rename of a binary file and a change of its mode alone stay in the patch, as headers with no
diff.
It leaves out, too, each changed file whose path begins with
.BR # ,
which
.IR patch (1)
would take for a device and refuse, naming it as
.BI "spots: device name: " path \f1;
only the first element counts, so
.B d/#n
stays in.
Such a path never pairs as a rename, so moving a file to or from one shows as a removal and a
whole new file: the half under
.B #
is always left out, and the other half reaches the patch only as text, since
.I spots
leaves out a binary one as binary.
It then exits with status
.BR binary ,
.BR "device name" ,
or both, so that a pipeline knows the patch is partial.
A file left out may hold the only copy of another file's text:
after
.BR "mv y x" ,
where
.B x
is binary, the patch removes
.B y
and
.I spots
names only
.BR x .
.PP
.I Spots
ends with an error when it cannot do its work in full.
When it cannot list the files that changed, because
.I walk
warns or a program it runs fails, it prints
.B "spots: cannot list the changes"
and exits with status
.BR list .
When it cannot look through a file for NUL bytes, because
.I freq
fails or warns, it prints
.B "spots: cannot look for NUL bytes"
and exits with status
.BR freq .
When
.I awk
or
.I diff
fails, it exits, before any patch for a failed
.IR diff ,
with status
.B awk
or
.BR diff .
.PP
.IR Cowfs
notes the SHA-1 of each file of the real tree as it copies it;
.I spots
warns of a file whose text has changed since then, or that has gone, as applying its patch would
undo that change.
The warning also catches a wrong
.I base
for the files cowfs copied or removed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Work in a shell, keeping its changes apart, then save them as a patch:
.IP
.EX
cow /tmp/e rc
spots /tmp/e | patch
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B rc/bin/cow
and
.B rc/bin/spots
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR diff (1),
.IR patch (1),
.IR cowfs (4)
.SH BUGS
Bytes past ASCII stay as they are in a name, as Git writes them with
.B core.quotepath
off.
.PP
.I Spots
leaves a file out whole, so when the path of a file it leaves out, or of a directory that holds
it, is a directory in one tree and a file in the other,
.I patch
refuses the whole patch.
.PP
.I Spots
finds a rename only when the text is the same;
a file renamed and changed shows as a removal and a creation.
